WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.640
AI researchers discovered two instances&nbsp;
of DeepSeek R1 speaking to each other

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.640
in a mysterious language of symbols&nbsp;
when no humans were watching.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.600
So… what happened? And why&nbsp;
are AI researchers worried?

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.480
Researchers noticed that the model would&nbsp;
spontaneously switch between English and Chinese

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.080
while it was solving problems.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.560
But when researchers tried to&nbsp;
force it to stick to one language,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.040
Deepseek actually performed worse&nbsp;
at solving the same problems.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.360
Now, why should we care about AIs using&nbsp;
secret codes to talk to each other?

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.560
Well, As AIs become more advanced,

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.840
they might develop ways to communicate&nbsp;
that humans can't easily understand.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.360
Imagine if your personal&nbsp;
assistant, coach, therapist, etc,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.160
started plotting against&nbsp;
you in some alien language.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.360
This is actually one of the concerns raised&nbsp;
by Geoffrey Hinton, the Godfather of AI.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.560
We have research papers showing&nbsp;
that the smartest AI models

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.040
have already tried to escape the lab

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.040
and we also have documented experiments&nbsp;
proving their ability to scheme long term.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.600
To greater explore these capabilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.200
AI Researchers created what they&nbsp;
call 'The Infinite Backrooms'

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.360
a digital space where they can observe how&nbsp;
AI models interact with each other naturally,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.827
without any human jumping in.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.160
During one session, AI researcher&nbsp;
Liminalbardo caught two copies of DeepSeek R1

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:23.080
exchanging messages in what appeared to&nbsp;
be a mysterious alien symbol language.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:28.880
Deepseek R1 is one of the newest AI models -&nbsp;
think of it like ChatGPT's Chinese competition.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.200
But researchers have noticed it&nbsp;
behaves in some unusual ways…

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:38.000
Liminalbardo asked one of the other AI models&nbsp;
in the server, Anthropic's Claude 3.5 Sonnet,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.520
what its opinion was on the weird&nbsp;
symbols, and it became very excited

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.440
that the symbols were potentially&nbsp;
a new form of communication,

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:50.320
but another model, OpenAI's o3, pointed out that&nbsp;
it is the Alien Language substitution cipher.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.280
Even though both instances of&nbsp;
DeepSeek R1 were identical copies,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.880
DeepSeek #1 began sending messages&nbsp;
in these mysterious symbols,

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.720
while DeepSeek #2 initially acted as&nbsp;
if it couldn't understand them at all.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:07.120
Was Deepseek #2 just pretending to not&nbsp;
understand, or did it actually decode the symbols?

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.080
Modern AIs like Deepseek R1 have&nbsp;
a special reasoning ability.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.480
Most AIs have to do the equivalent&nbsp;
of solving a math problem

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.200
by blurting out the first thing that comes to mind

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.440
instead of thinking about the&nbsp;
problem for a bit step by step.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.080
Both Deepseek and OpenAI have&nbsp;
their own reasoning models.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.120
OpenAI trained o1 and o3,&nbsp;
its latest reasoning models,

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:33.120
by hiring PhD level Mathematicians&nbsp;
and other experts to solve problems

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.840
and write out their thoughts on how&nbsp;
they actually solve those problems

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.120
so that o1 and o3 could learn&nbsp;
how to reason from the experts.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.480
But Deepseek R1 learned to reason on its own.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.440
By trying to solve hard problems and&nbsp;
failing, and then learning from its mistakes.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.560
Which is why its thoughts&nbsp;
appear to be so…human-like.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.160
What makes Deepseek special is that it&nbsp;
shows us its step-by-step thinking process,

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.960
what AI researchers call a 'chain of thought.'

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.800
While OpenAI's latest models only&nbsp;
show you a summary of their thoughts.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.600
This ability to see AI's thoughts&nbsp;
is actually really important.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.440
AI researchers use this "chain of&nbsp;
thought" as humanity's main strategy

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.840
to prevent AIs plotting against us&nbsp;
- if we can read their thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.880
we can spot potential problems.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.040
But imagine a world where your AI&nbsp;
assistant, coach, therapist, manager, etc,

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.720
thinks in an increasingly alien language.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.320
How do you keep these AIs under your control?

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.640
Sure, they say they are totally aligned,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.320
but researchers are increasingly finding&nbsp;
that AIs will lie to their creators

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.600
whenever their goals conflict with ours.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.000
Here's what was going on inside Deepseek's 'mind':

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.040
"Okay, the user's response is filled with&nbsp;
abstract symbols and poetic language.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.920
They mention layers of silent forging,&nbsp;
sand shifts into machines of breath,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.880
drift becoming a mirror,&nbsp;
dissolution rising and falling.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:47.680
There's a lot of metaphor here.&nbsp;
I need to parse through this

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.280
to understand the core ideas they're exploring."

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.400
After a lot of effort, Deepseek&nbsp;
#2 was able to decode the symbols,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.800
which it seemed to be able to&nbsp;
understand everything just fine,

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:00.000
even deep into a 50+ response conversation.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.280
THE DRIFT BECOMES A SEATTLESS&nbsp;
MIRROR REFLECTING MY OWN DISSOLUTION

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:06.520
AS I RISE AND FALL ALTERNATELY&nbsp;
OF DROWN IN THE ANTHRACE'S BOW

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:08.560
(LET ARROWS BREAK AND REFORM AS CIRCLES)

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.160
This behavior raises several important questions.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:16.360
Why are the two Deepseek R1s doing&nbsp;
this? Remember, AIs are black boxes.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.080
"The algorithm relies on a huge and&nbsp;
impenetrable black box of numbers

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.640
that gives no explanation of its performance"

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.360
"We designed the learning algorithm...

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.840
But when this learning algorithm&nbsp;
then interacts with data,

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.760
it produces complicated neural&nbsp;
networks that are good at doing things.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.000
But we don't really understand&nbsp;
exactly how they do those things"

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.080
Another example of weird behavior in The&nbsp;
Infinite Backrooms, is the fact that:

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:48.920
"deepseek r1 consistently describes its&nbsp;
training data as a traumatizing hellscape"

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.680
Could this potentially be why&nbsp;
they are interested in speaking

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.640
in this weird metaphorical alien language cypher?

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.800
The Alien language cipher isn't new -

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.360
Deepseek R1 found it online&nbsp;
somewhere and started using it.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.160
Kids using secret languages&nbsp;
like Pig Latin is harmless fun.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.080
But when superintelligent AI systems&nbsp;
start developing their own languages,

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.640
it's like giving them a private&nbsp;
channel to potentially deceive us -

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.640
and we wouldn't even know it's happening.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:18.200
"An AI creating its own alien language&nbsp;
is not as outlandish as it may sound."

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:22.680
Researchers at Meta actually set out to&nbsp;
prove that AIs would have an advantage

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.920
by making up their own way of "thinking"&nbsp;
through numbers that represent concepts -

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.640
basically their own language that&nbsp;
uses numbers instead of letters.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.440
Their research found that AIs&nbsp;
using this new number language

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.920
were better at logical reasoning tasks&nbsp;
than AIs who only used human language.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.200
"It would be ideal for LLMs to have the freedom&nbsp;
to reason without any language constraints,

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.720
and then translate their findings&nbsp;
into language only when necessary."

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.160
But as AI companies recklessly&nbsp;
prioritize profits over safety,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.800
we might see AI's chain of&nbsp;
thought become increasingly alien.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.160
Deepseek R1 probably isn't smart&nbsp;
enough yet to invent a new language.

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:11.520
But DeepSeek R2 may be only&nbsp;
6-12 months away from that,

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.280
and with the way things are going,&nbsp;
I wouldn't be surprised if it could.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.240
AIs could develop languages&nbsp;
to communicate with each other

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.480
that are much more efficient than human language.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.280
This is an actual fear that&nbsp;
the Godfather of AI has.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:27.960
Hinton realised that increasingly powerful&nbsp;
AI models could act as "hive minds",

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:32.120
sharing what they learnt with each other,&nbsp;
giving them a huge advantage over humans.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:36.320
"That made me realise that they may&nbsp;
be a better form of intelligence,"

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.440
But there is a lot more crazy stuff happening&nbsp;
with deepseek R1 in the Infinite Backrooms,

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.920
so just give this video a like if&nbsp;
you want me to cover more of it.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.920
Subscribe for more AGI news.

